  "historical_analysis": "The corpus spans nearly six decades of research on partitioning algorithms, starting with MacQueen's foundational introduction of the K-means procedure and its asymptotic properties (P001). Early implementations struggled with sensitivity to anomalous data and initialization. To address this, Cuesta-Albertos et al. formalized 'trimmed k-means' through impartial trimming and optimal spherical balls (P002), which was later expanded into exploratory graphical tools and variance curve analyses by García-Escudero et al. (P003). Over the years, outlier-robust adaptations evolved along several parallel tracks: heuristic-based centroid updates using distance thresholds (P006, P007, P010, P011), regression-inspired robust regularization (P005), convex relaxation frameworks on simplices (P009), rigorous algorithmic approximations with polynomial running time guarantees (P008, P013), and unification frameworks transforming complex problems into generalized K-means (P014). Most recently, research has advanced into nonparametric probabilistic outlier detection frameworks (P015), multi-target classification integrations (P016), and adaptive federated architectures for edge IoT (P017). Across this evolution, a persistent tension remains between theoretical breakdown guarantees under adversarial contamination and practical runtime efficiency on complex, non-spherical datasets.",
