    {
      "id": "G001",
      "gap": "Lack of deterministic, non-exhaustive algorithms for identifying optimal trimming regions without enforcing strict spherical symmetry and equal dispersion radii across clusters.",
      "why_it_matters": "Real-world datasets frequently contain clusters with varying densities, arbitrary non-spherical shapes, or heterogeneous dispersions. Assuming equal-radius spherical optimal trimming sets causes trimmed K-means and related variants to miss or poorly recover nearby or elongated clusters.",
      "what_is_known": "Theoretical work demonstrates that optimal trimming functions for alpha-trimmed K-means essentially coincide with indicator functions of unions of k balls of equal radius, which restricts effectiveness to spherical, well-separated clusters.",
      "what_remains_unknown": "How to formulate computationally efficient, non-exhaustive optimal trimming algorithms that accommodate heterogeneous, non-spherical cluster geometries and unequal dispersion radii without incurring prohibitive combinatorial complexity.",
      "gap_type": "explicit",
      "confidence": "high",
